<spell-notebook "section usage">
	<header level="1">
		Section
		<header>A section is a titled block of content that can close and keep its title in view.</>
	</>
	<section-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#heading-levels">Heading levels</>
				<ui-item link="#opening-and-closing">Opening and closing</>
				<ui-item link="#content-from-a-file">Content from a file</>
				<ui-item link="#sticky-titles">Sticky titles</>
				<ui-item link="#slots">Slots</>
				<ui-item link="#events">Events</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
				<ui-item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  the old Astro page's Behaviour, Accessibility and Framework usage, restyled. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Reach for a section when content needs a TITLE that means something to the page outline, and maybe closing or a
			title that stays in view. Its neighbours:

			| You want                                                     | Use                            |
			| ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------ |
			| a box or group of content, no title                          | [segment](/components/ui/ui-segment.html)     |
			| a heading on its own, styled                                 | [header](/components/ui/ui-header.html)       |
			| several panels where opening one may close the others        | [accordion](/components/ui/ui-accordion.html) |
			| a titled block that can close, stick, or both, in the outline | `<ui-section>`                 |
			| a run of titled blocks that all close, packed tight           | `<ui-sections collapsing>`     |
			| content that sticks, without a title of its own              | [sticky](/components/ui/ui-sticky.html)       |

			An accordion's titles are buttons, not headings, and its panels are one group; each section closes on its own and
			its title IS a heading. A section borrows Fomantic's looks for its box: `dividing` and `block` from the header,
			the `bordered` and `raised` looks, `attach` and `padding` from the segment, the `styled` look from the accordion.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Heading levels">
		<markdown>
			The title renders as `<h1>` ... `<h6>`.

			- Without `level`, a section takes its enclosing section's level + 1, and a top-level one is an `h2`,
			  under the page's `h1`. So nesting sections builds a correct outline by itself.
			- Set `level` where the section sits under a heading that isn't a section (as the examples on this page
			  do), or to restart the count.
			- Levels stop at `h6`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Opening and closing">
		<markdown>
			`collapsible` makes the title close and open the content.

			- `open` is CONTROLLED, as the accordion's is:  writing it closes or opens, and fires nothing.
			- When the USER activates the title, the cancelable `ui-opening` / `ui-closing` go first,
			  with `{ open, section, originalEvent }`. `preventDefault()` keeps things as they were.
			- It opens and closes with a height transition where the browser can animate to `auto` (`interpolate-size`);
			  with reduced motion it snaps.

			A closed section's content is `hidden="until-found"`:  it stays in the page.

			- So find-in-page (Ctrl / Cmd + F) finds text inside it, and opens the section to show the match.
			- That opening fires `ui-opening` with `cancelable: false`:
			  the browser has already revealed the match, so it can't be vetoed.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const section = document.querySelector("ui-section[collapsible]")
			section.addEventListener("ui-closing", (event) => {
			  if (hasUnsavedChanges) event.preventDefault() // stays open
			})
			section.open = true // open from code:  no event
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Content from a file">
		<markdown>
			`source` names an HTML file whose `<body>` becomes the content.
			So a long page can ship as titles alone, and load each body when it's read.

			- It's fetched the FIRST time the section opens:
			  by a click, by the page setting `open` (a `#id` link), or at once when it starts open.
			- The body goes in the section's LIGHT DOM, so the page's CSS styles it.
			  Whatever was there (except slotted title pieces) is a placeholder it replaces.
			- `select` takes only the first match of a CSS selector.
			- Same origin only, fetched and cached as [`<ui-include>`](/components/ui/ui-include.html) fetches:
			  scripts in the file don't run, and relative URLs are rewritten.

			While it loads, and after:

			- The content stays closed while the body is on its way, so the section opens once, onto the real content.
			- A slow file (over 300 ms) shows the placeholder under the `loading` look instead.
			- `ui-loaded` fires once the body is in (`:state(loaded)`).
			- A failure fires the cancelable `ui-error`, and shows an error line (`:state(error)`).
			  Opening again tries again.
			- `load()` fetches it now, closed or not;  `reload()` fetches it again past the cache, and replaces it.
		</>
		<example description="Open Chapter 2:  its content comes from `examples/chapter.html`.">
			<html>
				<ui-section label="Chapter 1" level="4" collapsible>
					<p>Written in the page.</p>
				</ui-section>
				<ui-section label="Chapter 2" level="4" source="/components/ui/examples/chapter.html" collapsible open="false">
					<p>Loading chapter 2…</p>
				</ui-section>
			</html>
		</example>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const section = document.querySelector("ui-section[source]")
			section.addEventListener("ui-loaded", (event) => console.log("loaded", event.detail.source))
			await section.load() // fetch and insert now, still closed
			await section.reload() // the file changed:  fetch it again and replace the content
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Sticky titles">
		<markdown>
			With `sticky`, the title bar sticks to the top of the scroll container while any of its section is on screen;
			the end of the section pushes it out.

			- A sticky section INSIDE another sticks just below its parent's title, so nested titles stack like breadcrumbs.
			- `offset` (pixels) moves a TOP-LEVEL section's stuck title down, e.g. below a fixed page header.
			  Nested sections work theirs out.
			- Only the title bar sticks:  the hint scrolls away.
			- `:state(stuck)` is set while it is stuck.

			Stuck titles cover what scrolls under them, so a stuck title reserves its room on the scroll container
			(`scroll-padding-top`), as [`<ui-sticky>`](/components/ui/ui-sticky.html) does.
			So Page Down, Space, focus and `scrollIntoView()` keep content out from under the stack,
			instead of hiding the next line behind it.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```css
			ui-section:state(stuck)::part(title) {
			  box-shadow: 0 2px 4px oklch(0 0 0 / 0.15);
			}
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Slots">
		<markdown>
			The default slot is the content:  anything, nested sections included.

			- `label`, `hint`, `description`, `icon` and `badge` each have an attribute for plain text,
			  and a slot for rich markup;  the slot wins.
			- `actions` has no attribute:  its controls sit at the right of the title bar, OUTSIDE the open / close button.
			  So clicking one never closes the section, and they stay visible while it's closed.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-opening` (cancelable) -- the user is opening the section: `{ open: true, section, originalEvent }`. Not
			  cancelable when find-in-page opens it.
			- `ui-closing` (cancelable) -- the user is closing the section: `{ open: false, section, originalEvent }`.
			- Writing `open` fires neither.
			- `ui-opened` / `ui-closed` -- it has opened or closed, by any route: the user, `open`, find-in-page.
			- `ui-loading` / `ui-loaded` -- a `source` body is on its way, then in place.

			The old names are gone:  `ui-opening` / `ui-closing` / `ui-loaded` are `ui-opening` / `ui-closing` /
			`ui-loaded`, and the attributes and slots `header`, `subhead` and `info` are `label`, `hint` and
			`description`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<html>
			<ui-table celled padding="compact" unstackable>
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Key</>
							<th>Action</>
						</>
					</>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td>Tab / Shift + Tab</>
							<td>Move to the open / close button, then the actions, then the content</>
						</>
						<tr>
							<td>Enter / Space</>
							<td>Close or open the focused section</>
						</>
					</>
				</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The title is a real heading, so sections appear in the page outline and screen readers can jump between them.
			- With `collapsible`, the heading CONTAINS the open / close button (`<hN><button aria-expanded>`), as the APG accordion
			  pattern does: the heading stays in the outline, and the button says whether the content is shown. The chevron
			  is decoration; the button's tooltip says "Close this section" or "Open this section" (translatable).
			- The actions are outside the button, so each one is its own tab stop with its own name.
			- Find-in-page reaches closed content and opens it.
			- `loading` sets `aria-busy` and announces "Loading…"; `disabled` makes the section inert.
			- Color is never the only signal: say in the title what a colored section means.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Bind `open` and listen for `ui-opened` / `ui-closed` to follow it, by any route.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-section
			  label="Details"
			  collapsible
			  open={isOpen}
			  onui-opened={() => setOpen(true)}
			  onui-closed={() => setOpen(false)}
			>
			  ...
			</ui-section>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-section
			  label="Details"
			  collapsible
			  :open="isOpen"
			  @ui-opened="isOpen = true"
			  @ui-closed="isOpen = false"
			>
			  ...
			</ui-section>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-section
			  label="Details"
			  collapsible
			  open={isOpen()}
			  on-ui-closed={() => setOpen(false)}
			>
			  ...
			</ui-section>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
